+		<h5 id="sage">Sage</h5>
+		<p>Choose one focused scholarly field. Broad fields must be narrowed to a culture, period, school, or subject. You know its common facts, methods, authorities, disputes, and language. You can recall ordinary information and use its tools without a throw.</p>
+		<p>You may interpret obscure sources, conduct original research, and locate answers. At the Referee's discretion, you may know libraries, schools, patrons, rivals, collectors, or forbidden archives.</p>
+		<p><em>Examples: Imperial funerary law, northern astronomy, pre-Conquest history, temple architecture, or one school of alchemy.</em></p>
+		<h5 id="tomb-robber">Tomb Robber</h5>
+		<p>You understand burial customs, grave goods, excavation, corpse preservation, funerary warnings, and relic markets. With tools and time, you can open ordinary graves, shore simple excavations, and recognize common signs of tampering without a throw.</p>
+		<p>You may infer where a burial chamber lies, recover fragile remains or treasure, or identify the guardian or curse a culture might employ. At the Referee's discretion, you may know gravediggers, embalmers, antiquarians, fences, priests, laborers, or families guarding ancestral secrets.</p>
+		<h4 id="feature-archetypes">Feature Archetypes</h4>
+		<p>Feature Archetypes add a signature rules feature to their narrative scope. Each is a separate Archetype and grants only the feature stated in its description.</p>
+		<aside class="referee-guidance" aria-label="Referee guidance">
+			<p class="referee-guidance__label">Referee Guidance</p>
+			<p>Offer Feature Archetypes that fit the campaign. Some depend on setting support: a Duelist needs recognized challenges, fighting schools, or rivals for much of its narrative scope to matter. Rename or reframe an Archetype when its feature fits but its institutions do not, and omit one whose feature or premise has no place in the campaign.</p>
+			<p>A renamed Feature Archetype retains its stated feature and changes only its narrative scope. See <a href="#archetype-variants">Archetype Variants</a> after the list.</p>
+		</aside>
+		<h5 id="warrior">Warrior</h5>
+		<p>You understand weapons, armor, formations, watches, camps, supply, morale, customs, and trained fighters. You can assess arms, organize a military camp, and drill willing combatants without a throw.</p>
+		<p>You may recognize tactics and negotiate with military groups. At the Referee's discretion, you may claim other warriors’ regard or hostility. Gain two additional Combat Moves for which you qualify.</p>
+		<h5 id="berserker">Berserker</h5>
+		<p>You understand warrior customs, weapons, boasts, feuds, and battle fury. You can distinguish ritual violence, intimidation, and intent to kill.</p>
+		<p>Once per battle, after losing Stamina but remaining conscious, you may enter a Rage. During it, ignore fear and penalties from pain or Wounds. You cannot cast spells, retreat voluntarily, or perform precise work. Rage ends when no immediate enemy remains, you fall unconscious, or an ally spends an action calming you. Deferred penalties then apply. At the Referee's discretion, you may know warriors, clan leaders, raiders, skalds, or survivors of past feuds.</p>
+		<h5 id="duelist">Duelist</h5>
+		<p>You understand dueling customs, challenges, footwork, weapon care, and fighting schools. You can identify an observed duelist’s skill and school when evidence permits.</p>
+		<p>Start with the Riposte Combat Move. It still requires Combat 3 to use. You may seek recognition, hospitality, instruction, or formal challenge from other duelists. At the Referee's discretion, schools bring rivals, debts, reputations, teachings, and duties.</p>
+		<h5 id="knight">Knight</h5>
+		<p>You understand heraldry, noble customs, horses, armor, fortifications, military duties, and warrior etiquette.</p>
