+		<aside class="referee-guidance" aria-label="Referee guidance">
+			<p class="referee-guidance__label">Referee Guidance</p>
+			<p>An Archetype grants knowledge and the possibility of specialized action, not automatic success under pressure. Require a throw when danger, haste, opposition, poor tools, or uncertain conditions make the outcome meaningful. It does not replace a Skill, equipment, or another Archetype’s distinct expertise.</p>
+			<p>A Tomb Robber and Burglar may both notice a funerary trap. The Tomb Robber is better at recognizing burial patterns and identifying the guardian or curse a culture might employ; the Burglar is better at understanding locks, triggers, alarms, and methods of bypass. Neither automatically disarms an unusual trap: suitable tools and a throw may be required. Tomb Robber alone does not grant general expertise with dungeons, unrelated ruins, arbitrary secret doors, ancient languages, monsters, or magic.</p>
+			<p>Use the same approach where other Archetypes overlap. Give each useful information from its own perspective, then apply normal throws and consequences.</p>
+		</aside>
+		<h3 id="archetype-variants">Archetype Variants</h3>
+		<p>A variant uses an existing Archetype under a setting-specific name. Record the variant followed by its functional Archetype in parentheses. It retains the Archetype’s rules feature, if any, and changes only the stated narrative scope.</p>
+		<p><strong>Gladiator (Warrior):</strong> Replace military formations, camps, and institutions with arenas, bouts, promoters, crowds, and rival fighters. Retain the Warrior’s two additional Combat Moves.</p>
+		<p><strong>Highwayman (Warrior):</strong> Replace military customs and institutions with roads, coaches, mounts, patrols, tolls, ambush sites, intimidation, and the habits of travelers. Retain the Warrior’s two additional Combat Moves.</p>
+		<p><strong>Runner (Courtier):</strong> Emphasize household offices, envoys, messages, pacing, routes, footwear, and relays over fashion and ceremony. You can maintain an ordinary courier’s pace and may attempt delivery through hostile territory, but gain no additional movement or wilderness expertise.</p>
+		<p><strong>Caravaner (Merchant):</strong> Choose overland trade and emphasize pack animals, wagons, cargo, camps, tolls, caravan discipline, and the practical work of keeping a caravan moving.</p>
+		<p><strong>Smuggler (Merchant):</strong> Choose illicit overland or overseas trade and emphasize contraband, customs searches, manifests, covert transfers, concealed cargo, and compromised officials. This does not grant the Intriguer’s expertise with forgery, false identities, or criminal politics.</p>
 		<h3 id="optional-rule-spellcaster-only-archetypes">Optional Rule: Only Sorcerers Can Use Sorcery</h3>
 		<p>Optionally, only a character with the Sorcerer Archetype and Lore 1+ may learn or cast spells from the general Spells lists. Characters with other Archetypes cannot choose general spells during character creation, gain them from Lore, or learn them from tomes. A Priest with Lore 1+ may still learn and cast divine spells.</p>
 		<p>This represents the difficult nature of spellcasting, magic, and the occult. Such characters are rare and specialized, and have dedicated years to studying sorcery.</p>
